    "summary": "Jam‑filled Fillmore show with scorching St. Stephen > Midnight Hour sequence",
    "blurb": "The June 4, 1970 performance delivers a monster St. Stephen > Midnight Hour > Not Fade Away run and a soulful take on It’s All Over Now Baby Blue. Jerry’s guitar tone is pristine, Bob’s Gibson ES shines in the mix, and the drums drive every transition. Acoustic highlights like Black Peter and Friend Of The Devil add depth to an already stellar set.",
    "review": "On June 4, 1970 at the Fillmore West the Dead were locked into a groove that still feels fresh today. The first set rolls through folk‑rock gems—Monkey And The Engineer, Deep Elem Blues, and a bright Black Peter—while Jerry’s sweet vocal on the acoustic numbers adds an intimate touch. The real fireworks erupt in the second set: a firecracker‑driven transition launches St. Stephen into a blistering jam that rockets straight into Not Fade Away, then morphs into a tight In The Midnight Hour before resolving with a heartfelt It’s All Over Now Baby Blue. Bob’s Gibson ES guitar is front‑and‑center, Pigpen’s organ adds a churchy edge to the jams, and Mickey’s crisp drumming (with Billy keeping solid time) fuels every surge. With multiple recordings capturing this night, the Miller soundboard tape stands out for its crystal‑clear mix, making it the definitive way to experience one of the Fillmore’s most celebrated shows.",
